1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,749
Ava: What if learning PyTorch internals meant building a tiny version yourself, on an old laptop, with no GPU?
Ava：如果只用一台没有 GPU 的旧笔记本，亲手做个小型框架，就能学会 PyTorch 内部原理呢？

2
00:00:07,749 --> 00:00:13,118
That’s TinyTorch, and it could change how framework engineers learn.
这就是 TinyTorch，它可能改变框架工程师的学习方式。

3
00:00:13,118 --> 00:00:16,720
Brian: Right. TinyTorch is a free, open-source curriculum.
Brian：没错。TinyTorch 是一套免费开源课程。

4
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:27,100
You build a working machine-learning framework from scratch, from tensors through transformers, in pure Python, while using PyTorch’s own API.
你会用纯 Python，从张量到 Transformer，依照 PyTorch 的 API，从零构建一个能运行的机器学习框架。

5
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:33,210
Ava: So this isn’t just another tutorial where you read about autograd and then import torch.
Ava：所以这不只是读完 autograd 自动求导原理，再导入 torch 的教程。

6
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:35,652
Brian: Exactly. You implement the pieces.
Brian：对。每个组件都要自己实现。

7
00:00:35,652 --> 00:00:43,256
There are twenty modules, and the whole thing runs on a laptop with four gigabytes of RAM and no GPU.
课程有 20 个模块，只需 4 GB 内存的笔记本，无需 GPU 就能运行。

8
00:00:43,256 --> 00:00:47,602
Ava: Why did the authors think PyTorch needed a teaching version?
Ava：作者为什么认为 PyTorch 需要一个教学版？

9
00:00:47,602 --> 00:00:51,646
PyTorch already has a huge amount of documentation.
PyTorch 的文档已经很丰富了。

10
00:00:51,646 --> 00:00:56,237
Brian: Because mature systems become too large to hold in your head.
Brian：因为成熟系统太庞大，没人能把它的全貌都装进脑子里。

11
00:00:56,237 --> 00:01:01,429
The article compares PyTorch with MINIX, the Tiger compiler, and xv6.
文章把 PyTorch 与 MINIX、Tiger 编译器和 xv6 作了比较。

12
00:01:01,429 --> 00:01:05,643
Those teaching systems don’t replace production systems.
这些教学系统不会取代生产系统。

13
00:01:05,643 --> 00:01:09,756
They expose what production systems normally hide.
它们让生产系统通常隐藏的机制显现出来。

14
00:01:09,756 --> 00:01:14,714
Ava: So TinyTorch is the missing rung below the real framework.
Ava：所以 TinyTorch 是通往真实框架的那级台阶。

15
00:01:14,714 --> 00:01:15,833
Brian: That’s the idea.
Brian：就是这个意思。

16
00:01:15,833 --> 00:01:24,017
Existing writing on PyTorch internals is often excellent, but it assumes you already think like a framework engineer.
现有的 PyTorch 内部原理文章往往写得很好，但默认你已经具备框架工程师的思维方式。

17
00:01:24,017 --> 00:01:28,984
TinyTorch gives you something you can finish and inspect yourself.
TinyTorch 则让你有个能亲手完成、亲自检查的系统。

18
00:01:28,984 --> 00:01:34,086
Ava: And the argument is aimed at PyTorch teams too, not only students.
Ava：而且文章也是写给 PyTorch 团队看的，不只是给学生。

19
00:01:34,086 --> 00:01:39,944
Brian: Yes. Frameworks depend on a small group of people who can reason from the inside.
Brian：对。框架离不开一小群能从内部理解系统的人。

20
00:01:39,944 --> 00:01:50,708
They notice tensor-cache leaks, understand when gradient checkpointing is worth recomputing, and identify bottlenecks before opening a profiler.
他们能发现张量缓存泄漏，判断何时值得用梯度检查点重新计算，还能在打开性能分析器前找出瓶颈。

21
00:01:50,708 --> 00:01:53,818
Ava: But that group doesn’t grow automatically.
Ava：但这样的人不会自动变多。

22
00:01:53,818 --> 00:01:57,968
Brian: Most people reach the internals because something broke badly.
Brian：大多数人是出了严重问题，才开始接触内部机制。

23
00:01:57,968 --> 00:02:04,058
They learn under deadline pressure, from the outside in, in whatever order the bug demands.
他们顶着期限压力，从外往里学，遇到什么故障就先学什么。

24
00:02:04,058 --> 00:02:07,152
That creates competence with holes in it.
这样练出的能力难免有缺口。

25
00:02:07,152 --> 00:02:10,790
Ava: Building the system changes that arrival path.
Ava：亲手构建系统，就改变了学习内部机制的路径。

26
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:16,243
Brian: Once you implement autograd yourself, you can’t unsee the computational graph.
Brian：一旦自己实现过 autograd 自动求导，就再也无法忽视计算图。

27
00:02:16,243 --> 00:02:21,556
Once you profile your own memory allocation, you can’t forget its cost.
一旦分析过自己程序的内存分配，就忘不了它的开销。

28
00:02:21,556 --> 00:02:27,330
Ava: The article has a nice image there: the abstraction can be a wall or a door.
Ava：文章里有个很妙的比喻：抽象层可以是一堵墙，也可以是一扇门。

29
00:02:27,330 --> 00:02:27,542
Brian: Right.
Brian：没错。

30
00:02:27,542 --> 00:02:31,431
A student who writes backward() and allocates Adam’s momentum and variance buffers already has a mental model.
自己写过 backward()、分配过 Adam 动量和方差缓冲区的学生，脑中已经有了模型。

31
00:02:31,431 --> 00:02:35,600
When they meet production autograd, it looks like a more sophisticated version of something they understand.
再接触生产级 autograd 时，他们看到的只是自己熟悉的机制变得更精密了。

32
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:38,374
Ava: That sounds useful for onboarding.
Ava：这对新人入职培训很有用。

33
00:02:38,374 --> 00:02:40,758
Brian: Companies are using it that way.
Brian：企业已经在这么用了。

34
00:02:40,758 --> 00:02:47,834
Teams run two-to-three-week intensives, quarter-long internal training, and targeted workshops.
团队会开设为期 2 到 3 周的强化班、持续一个季度的内部培训，以及专题工作坊。

35
00:02:47,834 --> 00:02:54,516
Someone might work through Module zero six on autograd or Module twelve on attention.
有人可能专门学习讲 autograd 自动求导的模块 06，或讲注意力机制的模块 12。

36
00:02:54,516 --> 00:02:58,202
Ava: Okay, how is the curriculum organized?
Ava：那课程是怎么安排的？

37
00:02:58,202 --> 00:03:03,288
Brian: Twenty modules in four tiers, driven by a command-line tool called tito.
Brian：分为 4 个层级，共 20 个模块，通过名为 tito 的命令行工具推进。

38
00:03:03,288 --> 00:03:09,712
The lessons are Jupyter notebooks with the hard parts removed for learners to fill in.
课程使用 Jupyter 笔记本，把难点留空，让学习者自己补上。

39
00:03:09,712 --> 00:03:11,766
Ava: What does a learner need?
Ava：学习者需要什么基础？

40
00:03:11,766 --> 00:03:14,221
Brian: Python and comfort with NumPy.
Brian：会用 Python，熟悉 NumPy。

41
00:03:14,221 --> 00:03:20,708
No GPU, no cloud account, and no prior machine-learning systems background.
不需要 GPU、云账户，也不需要机器学习系统方面的基础。

42
00:03:20,708 --> 00:03:25,042
Ava: The first design choice is called systems from day one, right?
Ava：第一个设计选择叫“从第一天就学系统”，对吧？

43
00:03:25,042 --> 00:03:31,956
Brian: Yes. Module zero one includes a memory_footprint() method before matrix multiplication.
Brian：对。模块 01 在讲矩阵乘法之前，就加入了 memory_footprint() 方法。

44
00:03:31,956 --> 00:03:39,936
You learn that one batch of thirty-two ImageNet images costs nineteen megabytes by computing it.
你会亲手算出，一批 32 张 ImageNet 图像要占 19 MB 内存。

45
00:03:39,936 --> 00:03:46,022
Ava: Then later you discover Adam’s memory cost by actually allocating its buffers.
Ava：后来，你还会通过实际分配缓冲区，发现 Adam 的内存开销。

46
00:03:46,022 --> 00:03:51,269
Brian: Exactly. Adam needs roughly three times the optimizer memory of SGD.
Brian：没错。Adam 所需的优化器内存大约是 SGD 的 3 倍。

47
00:03:51,269 --> 00:03:57,484
You measure that yourself, so it’s harder to dismiss as a fact from a slide.
你自己量过，就不会把它当成幻灯片上的一个数字。

48
00:03:57,484 --> 00:04:01,002
Ava: The second choice is progressive disclosure.
Ava：第二个选择是逐步揭示细节。

49
00:04:01,002 --> 00:04:03,666
Brian: The Tensor class stays clean through Module zero five.
Brian：直到模块 05，Tensor 类都保持简洁。

50
00:04:03,666 --> 00:04:10,232
In Module zero six, you implement enable_autograd(), adding requires_grad, grad, and backward() to the class you already know.
Brian：到了模块 06，你要实现 enable_autograd()，为熟悉的类加入 requires_grad、grad 和 backward()。

51
00:04:10,232 --> 00:04:16,486
Ava: That keeps the early data layout and arithmetic from being buried under gradient machinery.
Ava：这样，前面的数据布局和运算就不会被梯度机制淹没。

52
00:04:16,486 --> 00:04:17,142
Brian: And the old code keeps working.
Brian：旧代码也照样能用。

53
00:04:17,142 --> 00:04:18,996
The implementation uses runtime monkey-patching instead of inheritance.
实现方式是在运行时动态修改类，而不是使用继承。

54
00:04:18,996 --> 00:04:23,234
Ava: That choice will make some Python people uncomfortable.
Ava：有些 Python 开发者可能不喜欢这个选择。

55
00:04:23,234 --> 00:04:23,406
Brian: The authors admit that.
Brian：作者也承认这一点。

56
00:04:23,406 --> 00:04:24,449
It won because one Tensor class survives across all twenty modules, and students remember the moment their tensors visibly gain new powers.
之所以这样选，是因为同一个 Tensor 类贯穿全部 20 个模块，而且学生会记住张量明显获得新能力的那一刻。

57
00:04:24,449 --> 00:04:25,384
It also mirrors PyTorch zero point four, when Variable was merged into Tensor.
这也呼应了 PyTorch 0.4 将 Variable 合并进 Tensor 的做法。

58
00:04:25,384 --> 00:04:28,062
Ava: What’s the third design choice?
Ava：第三个设计选择是什么？

59
00:04:28,062 --> 00:04:29,150
Brian: Build to validate.
Brian：通过动手构建来验证。

60
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:48,476
Six historical milestones check whether the implementation works: the Perceptron in nineteen fifty-eight, the XOR crisis in nineteen sixty-nine, the backpropagation revival in nineteen eighty-six, a nineteen ninety-eight CNN breakthrough, the Transformer in twenty seventeen, and finally MLPerf-style benchmarking.
六个历史里程碑用来检验实现是否有效：1958 年的感知机、1969 年的 XOR 危机、1986 年反向传播的复兴、1998 年 CNN 的突破、2017 年的 Transformer，最后是 MLPerf 式基准测试。

61
00:04:48,476 --> 00:04:54,514
Ava: So history supplies motivation, but task performance supplies evidence.
Ava：也就是说，历史提供动机，任务表现提供证据。

62
00:04:54,514 --> 00:04:58,455
Brian: Exactly. A unit test can be faked or misunderstood.
Brian：没错。单元测试可能被敷衍通过，也可能被误解。

63
00:04:58,455 --> 00:05:03,865
If your network learns, your autograd has to be doing something right.
如果网络真的学会了，你的自动求导至少有些地方是对的。

64
00:05:03,865 --> 00:05:10,320
For the CNN milestone, the network must clear seventy-five percent on CIFAR-10.
在 CNN 这一关，网络在 CIFAR-10 上的准确率必须超过 75%。

65
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:14,102
Ava: And the API deliberately looks like PyTorch.
Ava：而且 API 是特意仿照 PyTorch 设计的。

66
00:05:14,102 --> 00:05:16,282
Brian: That’s the transfer mechanism.
Brian：这就是迁移机制。

67
00:05:16,282 --> 00:05:23,404
If you build loss.backward() in TinyTorch, you can recognize the shape of PyTorch’s version later.
如果你在 TinyTorch 中实现 loss.backward()，以后就能看懂 PyTorch 版本的结构。

68
00:05:23,404 --> 00:05:30,076
A PyTorch developer can read the attention module and see how the graph is constructed.
PyTorch 开发者读注意力模块时，也能看出计算图是怎么构建的。

69
00:05:30,076 --> 00:05:33,978
Ava: But the resemblance stops at the API surface.
Ava：但相似之处只到 API 这一层。

70
00:05:33,978 --> 00:05:40,594
Brian: Yes. There’s no dispatcher, no C++ or CUDA layer, no JIT, and nothing distributed.
Brian：对。它没有调度器、C++ 或 CUDA 层，也没有 JIT 和分布式功能。

71
00:05:40,594 --> 00:05:49,496
It’s also slow: pure Python runs somewhere between one hundred and ten thousand times slower than PyTorch.
它也很慢：纯 Python 实现比 PyTorch 慢约 100 到 10000 倍。

72
00:05:49,496 --> 00:05:51,790
Ava: That range is huge.
Ava：这个范围可真大。

73
00:05:51,790 --> 00:05:55,370
Brian: The article gives the range without narrowing it.
Brian：文章只给出了这个范围，没有进一步缩小。

74
00:05:55,370 --> 00:05:58,803
The slowness became part of the lesson, though.
不过，慢也成了教学的一部分。

75
00:05:58,803 --> 00:06:05,436
A TinyTorch Conv2d can take ninety-seven seconds where PyTorch takes ten milliseconds.
TinyTorch 的 Conv2d 可能要跑 97 秒，而 PyTorch 只要 10 毫秒。

76
00:06:05,436 --> 00:06:09,314
Ava: That makes vectorization painfully concrete.
Ava：这让向量化的必要性变得切身体会得到。

77
00:06:09,314 --> 00:06:15,568
Brian: Exactly. It stops being advice you read and becomes something that happened to you.
Brian：没错。它不再是读来的建议，而是亲身经历。

78
00:06:15,568 --> 00:06:18,006
Ava: Where did the project come from?
Ava：这个项目是怎么来的？

79
00:06:18,006 --> 00:06:26,223
Brian: It began as a Harvard course, CS two forty-nine r, launched in twenty twenty as a graduate seminar on TinyML.
Brian：它始于哈佛 2020 年开设的研究生研讨课 CS 249r，主题是 TinyML。

80
00:06:26,223 --> 00:06:30,644
There was no textbook, so the course notes became one.
当时没有教材，课程讲义后来就成了教材。

81
00:06:30,644 --> 00:06:34,402
Ava: Then an open repository grew around the notes.
Ava：随后，讲义周围逐渐形成了一个开放仓库。

82
00:06:34,402 --> 00:06:38,556
Brian: Students and educators fixed examples and proposed chapters.
Brian：学生和教育工作者修正示例，也提议增加章节。

83
00:06:38,556 --> 00:06:46,176
By twenty twenty-four, people kept asking about training at scale, so the project split into two volumes.
到 2024 年，不断有人询问大规模训练，项目于是分成了两卷。

84
00:06:46,176 --> 00:06:49,694
Ava: And eventually a textbook wasn’t enough.
Ava：后来，光有教材也不够了。

85
00:06:49,694 --> 00:06:55,242
Brian: Reading about autograd and implementing autograd create different kinds of knowledge.
Brian：阅读自动求导和亲手实现自动求导，获得的是两种不同的知识。

86
00:06:55,242 --> 00:07:07,732
The project added build modules, Marimo labs, MLSys dot im for performance modeling, hardware kits for Arduino and Raspberry Pi, StaffML for interview preparation, and an instructor hub.
项目又加入了动手构建模块、Marimo 实验、用于性能建模的 MLSys.im、Arduino 和 Raspberry Pi 硬件套件、用于面试准备的 StaffML，以及教师中心。

87
00:07:07,732 --> 00:07:11,586
Ava: The community numbers also changed quickly.
Ava：社区规模也迅速变化。

88
00:07:11,586 --> 00:07:17,138
Brian: The article says there were about two thousand stars in August twenty twenty-five.
Brian：文章说，2025 年 8 月时，仓库大约有 2000 颗星。

89
00:07:17,138 --> 00:07:20,252
Someone posted the repository on X in October.
有人在 10 月把仓库分享到 X。

90
00:07:20,252 --> 00:07:33,872
Two weeks later it reached five thousand, passed ten thousand by December, and is now above twenty-seven thousand, with at least ninety-five contributors and courses at fifty or more universities.
两周后星数达到 5000，12 月突破 10000，如今已超过 27000；贡献者至少有 95 人，50 多所大学开设了相关课程。

91
00:07:33,872 --> 00:07:37,702
Ava: They’re clear that this wasn’t a clever growth campaign.
Ava：他们也说得很清楚，这并不是什么精心策划的增长活动。

92
00:07:37,702 --> 00:07:43,383
Brian: Right. One person with reach shared it, after five years of quiet accumulation.
Brian：对。默默积累了五年后，一位有影响力的人分享了它。

93
00:07:43,383 --> 00:07:48,488
Andrea now maintains it from ETH Zurich, after it was built at Harvard.
这个项目在哈佛创建，如今由 Andrea 在苏黎世联邦理工学院维护。

94
00:07:48,488 --> 00:07:57,036
Moving a curriculum between institutions is a real test of whether it serves people beyond its original classroom.
一套课程换了学校还能用，才真正检验它能否服务原来课堂以外的人。

95
00:07:57,036 --> 00:08:02,690
Ava: The article gives four lessons for other open curricula. Let’s walk through them.
Ava：文章为其他开放课程总结了四条经验。我们逐条聊聊。

96
00:08:02,690 --> 00:08:05,684
Brian: First, match the production API exactly.
Brian：第一，教学 API 要与实际生产环境的 API 完全一致。

97
00:08:05,684 --> 00:08:13,144
Every hour learners spend translating between a teaching API and the real one is wasted effort.
学习者每花一小时在教学 API 和真实 API 之间转换，都是白费力气。

98
00:08:13,144 --> 00:08:17,814
Ava: Second, choose the hardware floor before the feature list.
Ava：第二，先确定最低硬件要求，再列功能清单。

99
00:08:17,814 --> 00:08:25,138
Brian: TinyTorch targets a dual-core two-gigahertz CPU, four gigabytes of RAM, and no network during training.
Brian：TinyTorch 面向双核 2 GHz CPU、4 GB 内存的设备，训练时无需联网。

100
00:08:25,138 --> 00:08:37,220
It ships two tiny offline datasets: about one thousand grayscale digits and three hundred fifty conversational question-answer pairs, together under fifty megabytes.
它附带两份很小的离线数据集：约 1000 张灰度数字图像和 350 组对话问答，总共不到 50 MB。

101
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:44,170
Ava: That choice excludes GPU support, but it enables Chromebook classrooms and old laptops.
Ava：这个选择放弃了 GPU 支持，却让 Chromebook 教室和旧笔记本也能用。

102
00:08:44,170 --> 00:08:44,452
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

103
00:08:44,452 --> 00:08:52,704
Decide whom you’re willing to exclude before the project grows, because revisiting that decision gets expensive.
项目壮大前，就要想清楚愿意让谁无法使用；以后再改，代价会很高。

104
00:08:52,704 --> 00:08:56,486
Ava: Third, slow can be educational.
Ava：第三，慢也能教会人东西。

105
00:08:56,486 --> 00:09:03,652
Brian: The ninety-seven-second convolution teaches the need for vectorization better than a warning ever could.
Brian：一次耗时 97 秒的卷积，比任何警告都更能让人明白为什么需要向量化。

106
00:09:03,652 --> 00:09:08,514
Ava: And fourth, instructor infrastructure is the real bottleneck.
Ava：第四，教师配套才是真正的瓶颈。

107
00:09:08,514 --> 00:09:13,430
Brian: Good content alone doesn’t get adopted. Gradeable content does.
Brian：光有好内容还不够。内容能评分，才容易被采用。

108
00:09:13,430 --> 00:09:27,200
TinyTorch includes NBGrader autograding, locked test cells, point allocations, an INSTRUCTOR.md, milestone scripts, and three integration models for different course sizes.
TinyTorch 包含 NBGrader 自动评分、锁定的测试单元格、分值分配、INSTRUCTOR.md、里程碑脚本，以及适用于不同课程规模的三种集成模式。

109
00:09:27,200 --> 00:09:32,230
Ava: So the instructor has to be supported as carefully as the learner.
Ava：所以，教师也需要得到和学生同样周到的支持。

110
00:09:32,230 --> 00:09:37,331
Brian: Exactly. The instructor must defend the curriculum to a committee.
Brian：正是。教师还得向委员会说明这套课程为什么值得采用。

111
00:09:37,331 --> 00:09:41,700
If that path fails, teaching quality doesn’t matter.
如果过不了这一关，教学质量再高也没用。

112
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:44,402
Ava: There’s also a succession plan.
Ava：项目还有接班计划。

113
00:09:44,402 --> 00:09:44,663
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

114
00:09:44,663 --> 00:09:56,420
The project commits to maintenance through twenty twenty-seven, a two-week pull-request review target, and a governance transition during twenty twenty-six and twenty twenty-seven.
项目承诺维护到 2027 年，争取在两周内审核拉取请求，并在 2026 至 2027 年间完成治理权交接。

115
00:09:56,420 --> 00:10:01,408
Sustainability means making yourself replaceable, with a date attached.
可持续发展，就是定下日期，让别人能接替自己。

116
00:10:01,408 --> 00:10:03,702
Ava: What remains unknown?
Ava：还有哪些未知数？

117
00:10:03,702 --> 00:10:09,373
Brian: TinyTorch is still in preview, aimed at classroom readiness for Fall twenty twenty-six.
Brian：TinyTorch 仍处于预览阶段，目标是在 2026 年秋季达到课堂使用标准。

118
00:10:09,373 --> 00:10:12,502
The authors have not measured learning outcomes.
作者还没有衡量学习成效。

119
00:10:12,502 --> 00:10:23,804
They have a strong educational rationale, but no controlled data showing that TinyTorch students debug production systems better than students in a conventional course.
他们有充分的教育理念依据，但没有对照数据证明，TinyTorch 学生比传统课程的学生更擅长排查生产系统的问题。

120
00:10:23,804 --> 00:10:27,082
Ava: And the technical scope has real holes.
Ava：技术范围也有明显缺口。

121
00:10:27,082 --> 00:10:29,192
Brian: It’s single-node and CPU-only.
Brian：它只支持单节点和 CPU。

122
00:10:29,192 --> 00:10:36,364
It teaches memory and compute, but not GPU kernels, distributed training, or gradient synchronization.
它讲内存和计算，但不讲 GPU 内核、分布式训练或梯度同步。

123
00:10:36,364 --> 00:10:46,488
Parallel data loading and GPU memory management were deliberately left out because doing them properly would break the four-gigabyte floor.
并行数据加载和 GPU 内存管理被有意排除，因为要把它们做好，就无法维持最低 4 GB 的硬件门槛。

124
00:10:46,488 --> 00:10:49,598
Ava: So what can people do next?
Ava：大家接下来能做什么？

125
00:10:49,598 --> 00:11:05,212
Brian: They can review modules against real PyTorch semantics, pilot a tier and report failures, write the missing distributed and GPU modules, localize the material, or adopt it and add their institution to the community map.
Brian：可以对照 PyTorch 的实际语义审查模块，试用一个课程层级并反馈问题，编写缺失的分布式和 GPU 模块，翻译本地化教材，或采用这套课程并把自己的机构加入社区地图。

126
00:11:05,212 --> 00:11:12,018
Ava: The code is MIT licensed, and the curriculum is CC BY-SA four point zero.
Ava：代码采用 MIT 许可证，课程内容采用 CC BY-SA 4.0 许可证。

127
00:11:12,018 --> 00:11:15,731
Brian: Right. Forking and adapting are explicitly allowed.
Brian：对。项目明确允许复刻和改编。

128
00:11:15,731 --> 00:11:27,728
The project also invites people to join the PyTorch Academic OSPO Working Group, which connects researchers, educators, students, and open-source practitioners.
项目也邀请大家加入 PyTorch Academic OSPO Working Group，让研究人员、教育工作者、学生和开源实践者相互交流。

129
00:11:27,728 --> 00:11:31,033
Ava: Let’s close with the three points I’m taking away.
Ava：最后说说我记住的三点。

130
00:11:31,033 --> 00:11:36,670
First, building a small framework creates a mental model for the production one.
第一，亲手构建一个小框架，能帮助你理解生产级框架。

131
00:11:36,670 --> 00:11:43,884
Brian: Second, matching the real API and keeping the hardware floor low makes the curriculum transferable.
Brian：第二，与真实 API 保持一致、降低硬件门槛，课程才能迁移到实际应用中。

132
00:11:43,884 --> 00:11:52,010
Ava: Third, the project is promising but still needs evidence, instructors, and contributors for the parts it leaves out.
Ava：第三，项目很有前景，但仍需要学习成效的证据、教师的参与，以及贡献者补上缺失的部分。

133
00:11:52,010 --> 00:11:55,446
Brian: That’s TinyTorch: don’t just import PyTorch.
Brian：这就是 TinyTorch：别只会导入 PyTorch。

134
00:11:55,446 --> 00:12:01,576
Build enough of it to understand what happens when the abstraction breaks.
亲手构建足够多的部分，才能明白抽象失效时会发生什么。

135
00:12:01,576 --> 00:12:04,998
Ava: Thanks for listening. We’ll see you next time.
Ava：感谢收听。下次见。
